{
  "_comment": "Which screen overlay belongs to which Vectrex game. Every retail Vectrex cartridge shipped with a translucent colored sheet in front of the screen, and this file is the box it came in: rows map a game to its sheet, matched by RomM's md5 first (filename-proof) and by a normalized name token as the fallback for dumps the hash list has never seen. Resolution picks the row whose matched token is longest, so '3d mine storm' beats 'mine storm'; a winning row with historical=false renders nothing, which is how the invented sheets (prototypes, the 3D Imager games, homebrew hacks) both stay out of the picture and block their name-collisions from borrowing a real game's sheet. The 3D Imager games colored their picture through spinning wheels in the headset, not screen sheets, and the prototypes never shipped, so their community-made overlays are bundled but inactive. Mine Storm II is the one deliberate cross-mapping: GCE mailed it as a bare bug-fix cart meant to be played under the console's original Mine Storm sheet, so its md5 points at that sheet rather than the invented Mine_Storm_II art. Art from libretro/overlay-borders (credits THK and Gigapig of the Hyperspin community); see docs/licenses.md.",
